  decides whether there's anything observable to test end-to-end, and whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m/[38;5;153mapp.js[39m get touched at all.

  - [1mStorage layer only[0m — buys: one small focused module with a clear interface, testable on its own, no UI churn. Costs:
    nothing in the app reads or writes it yet, so "settings persist across sessions" isn't demonstrably true until a
    later change uses it.
  - [1mStorage layer + one starter preference[0m — buys: a working end-to-end path you can verify by reloading the page.
    Costs: requires adding a settings control to a page that currently has only a login form. Cheap to extend afterward.
  - [1mStorage layer + a small settings section[0m — buys: an obvious home for future preferences. Costs: most of the work
    becomes UI for settings that don't exist yet — largely speculative.

  [1mMy recommendation: storage layer plus one starter preference.[0m It keeps the module small while making the persistence
  claim verifiable. A "remember my username" checkbox on the existing form is the natural fit, and I'd store only the
  username — never the password.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should this change cover? → Storage + one preference (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more question before I present the design. The repo has no configured tooling — no linter, no formatter, no test
  runner — so there's currently no way to test a preferences module beyond loading the page by hand. Setting this up is
  cheapest now, before the code exists, and whatever you pick lands in the spec's constraints so later work inherits it.

